.TH AGRF 1 "2026-09-03" "agrf" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agrf \- numbers in, braille sparkline out
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agrf
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[\fIVALUES\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agrf
reads whitespace\-separated integers or floats from positional
arguments (if any) or standard input and draws them as a compact
sparkline / bar chart using braille dots (U+2800), or block eighths
(U+2581..U+2588) where the font has no braille.
.PP
Each value maps to one pixel column; two columns fit in one braille
character, so a graph
.I W
characters wide shows the last 2*\fIW\fR values, filled left to right.
Non\-numeric tokens become gaps (empty columns). The vertical scale runs
from
.B \-\-min
(default 0) to
.B \-\-max
(default: the window maximum); values outside the range clamp to it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-width " \fIN\fR"
Output width in characters; holds 2*\fIN\fR values, or \fIN\fR with
.BR \-\-charset " " blocks .
Default: 20.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-height " \fIN\fR"
Output height in characters; 4 pixels per character, or 8 with
.BR \-\-charset " " blocks .
Default: 1.
.TP
.BR \-\-min " \fIF\fR|" auto
Y\-axis minimum; values below are clamped. Default: 0. With
.B auto
the floor is the smallest value in the window, which spends the whole
height on the variation rather than on the distance from zero \(en useful
for data that lives in a band, such as a temperature between 40 and 60.
Must be finite: a non\-numeric or infinite floor is rejected rather than
silently producing a blank graph.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max " \fIF\fR"
Y\-axis maximum; values above are clamped. Default: auto (window max).
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-point
Point mode: draw only the marker at each value, without filling
underneath. Default: bars.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-follow
Follow standard input: redraw after every line instead of reading to
end\-of\-file first. On a terminal each frame overwrites the previous one;
elsewhere the frames are written one after another, since a pipe has no
cursor to move and a reader wants the history. Values that scroll out of
the window are discarded, so following an endless stream does not grow
without bound. Cannot be combined with positional values, which are not a
stream.
.IP
Every program upstream in the pipe must flush per line. When stdout is
not a terminal, libc switches it from line\-buffered to fully buffered, so
a tool that does not flush holds its output until 4 KB has accumulated \(en
at ten samples a second, minutes \(en and
.B \-\-follow
sits there with nothing to draw. Use
.BR "jq \-\-unbuffered" ,
.BR "grep \-\-line\-buffered" ,
.BR "sed \-u" ,
.B fflush()
in awk, or
.B stdbuf \-oL
in front of anything else. To find the culprit, put
.B | cat
in front of
.BR agrf :
if that prints nothing either, the buffering is upstream.
.TP
.BR \-\-charset " " braille | blocks
Glyphs to draw with. Default:
.BR braille ,
which packs two values into each character (2 px wide by 4 px tall).
.B blocks
draws one value per character over eight vertical levels \(en half the
horizontal resolution for twice the vertical, and the fallback for a
terminal whose font has no braille. Because the block glyphs can only
express a fill from the bottom,
.B \-\-point
cannot be combined with them and the pair is rejected.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Plot a sequence across the default width:
.RS
.EX
seq 1 100 | agrf
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ping latency as a sparkline, two rows tall:
.RS
.EX
ping \-c 20 1.1.1.1 | grep \-oP 'time=\eK[0-9.]+' | agrf \-w 20 \-H 2
.EE
.RE
.PP
Positional values in point mode:
.RS
.EX
agrf \-p 3 7 2 9 5
.EE
.RE
.PP
Live ping latency, ten samples a second, redrawn as each reply arrives:
.RS
.EX
fping \-p 100 1.1.1.1 \-lJ | jq '.resp.rtt' \-\-unbuffered | agrf \-f \-H 5 \-w 64
.EE
.RE
.PP
A band of temperatures, scaled to its own range, in blocks:
.RS
.EX
agrf \-\-min auto \-\-charset blocks 41 43 47 44 52 49
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success \(en including
.B \-\-follow
reaching end\-of\-file \(en 1 on an I/O error (standard input cannot be read,
or the output cannot be written), and 2 on invalid arguments. A closed
output pipe, as in
.BR "agrf | head" ,
is not an error and returns 0.
.SH SEE ALSO
Project page: https://github.com/rmrfus/agrf
